# heap.cyr -- the allocation scope: where every allocation hisab makes goes (3.4.2)
# Usage: shipped inside dist/hisab.cyr; in-tree, include "src/heap.cyr" after what it requires
# Requires: no other hisab module (stdlib resolves via cyrius.cyml [deps])
#
# hisab returns fresh values (every HVec3, HQuat, matrix and vec result) and builds its working
# sets on the heap. Through 3.4.1 all of it came from `alloc`, the stdlib's global bump heap,
# which has no free, and a caller cannot reclaim it either: `alloc_reset()` frees the caller's
# own live blocks too. So a consumer that calls hisab once per frame, step or request grew the
# heap without bound: 24 B a `hvec3_add`, 105,920 B a `gjk_epa_3d` on two spheres, about 10 MB
# a step for 100 touching pairs (issue 2026-11-09-allocator-scoped-calls, impetus H9, pravash).
# [measured: docs/development/issues/archived/2026-11-09-allocator-scoped-calls-repro.cyr, on 3.4.1]
#
# ⭐ THE SCOPE. `hisab_alloc_scope(al)` routes every allocation hisab's own code makes, until
# the next call, to the Allocator `al` (lib/alloc.cyr's vtable: `arena_allocator_growable(n)`,
# `arena_allocator(n)`, or any `allocator_new`), and `hisab_alloc_scope(0)` routes them back to
# the global heap. It returns the scope it replaced, so scopes nest. A per-step consumer:
#
#     var step = arena_allocator_growable(1 << 20);   # once
#     ...
#     var prev = hisab_alloc_scope(step);             # every step
#     ... hisab calls ...
#     hisab_alloc_scope(prev);
#     ... copy out what the step keeps ...
#     reset_via(step);                                # the step's hisab memory, all of it
#
# A growable arena keeps its chunks across `reset_via`, so after the first steps a step
# allocates nothing new from the global heap (tests/modules_c.tcyr, group "3.4.2: the
# allocation scope").
#
# WHAT IT COVERS: every `alloc` site in src/ (hisab_alloc), every vec hisab creates or grows
# (hisab_vec_new, vec_push_a(hisab_allocator(), ...)), and every Str the symbolic modules
# build. WHAT IT DOES NOT: memory ganita's own routines take (`ganita_mat_new`,
# `ganita_mat_copy`, `ganita_mat_svd` and `ganita_mat_lu_solve` allocate with `alloc` inside
# ganita, and ganita's matrix is ganita's to lay out). Eight public functions reach them and
# still take global heap under a scope, and the ganita matrices they return live there:
# `inertia_box`, `inertia_sphere`, `mat_new_guarded`, `svd_compute_checked`, `svd_truncated`
# (linalg_ext), `eigen_qr`, `svd_golub_kahan` (linalg_precision) and
# `opt_levenberg_marquardt` (optimize). Nothing else in hisab calls an allocating stdlib
# function; scripts/check-heap-globals.sh refuses a new direct one. And memory a callback of
# the caller's allocates (a GJK support function, an ODE right-hand side) is the caller's:
# under a scope, its own `hvec3_*` calls are scoped, its own `alloc` calls are not.
# Of tests/hisab.bcyr's 375 calls, each run under a scope, 364 took 0 B of global heap, 9 reached
# the eight above and 2 allocate in the benchmark function itself.
# [measured: scratch sweep, tests/hisab.bcyr with bench() replaced by a scope check, 2026-11-14]
#
# ⚠ WHAT A RESET INVALIDATES: every value hisab returned under the scope, and every structure
# hisab GREW under it. A spatial hash, k-d tree, octree, BVH, tape or vec created outside the
# scope and inserted into inside it keeps its new storage in the scope's allocator, so the
# reset leaves it pointing at reused memory. Build and grow long-lived structures outside a
# scope you reset (or in a scope of their own), and copy out the values a step keeps. The same
# contract as `alloc_reset()`, narrowed to what hisab allocated while the scope was set.
# ⚠ ONE SCOPE PER PROCESS, as hisab's module state is: a scope set on one thread is the scope
# of every thread's hisab calls. Do not set or reset a scope from inside a callback hisab is
# running (a support function, an operator): the call's earlier allocations came from the
# scope it started with.
#
# EVERY ALLOCATION READS AS `alloc`'s DID: n > 0 bytes, zeroed (alloc hands out fresh or
# reset-zeroed memory, and hisab code relies on it, so memory from the scope's allocator is
# zeroed here, n bytes, as an arena does not zero what it re-issues); 0 for n <= 0, for
# n > ALLOC_MAX, and when the allocator refuses, which every hisab function already answers
# with its designed failure (3.4.1's refused-allocation work). The default path, no scope,
# is `alloc` itself.

var _hsb_al = 0;   # the caller's Allocator in scope, or 0: the global bump heap

# ⚡ THE DEFAULT PATH IS A CALL OF `alloc` THROUGH A POINTER, inlined. `#inline` refuses a body with
# control flow, so hisab_alloc cannot test the scope and stay inlined. The first form (a test, then
# alloc) cost every allocating call an extra frame: vec3_add 17 -> 25 ns, quat_rotate_vec3 52 -> 62,
# m3_mul_vec3 41 -> 46, over the 375 rows a median +2.1%. Instead hisab_alloc_scope swaps these two
# pointers, and hisab_alloc / hisab_allocator are one indirect call each, at the caller.
# [measured: scratch A/B, tests/hisab.bcyr, 3.4.1 tag and the first 3.4.2 form, 3 runs each interleaved, 2026-11-14]
var _hsb_fp = &alloc;           # the allocation: alloc, or _hsb_scoped under a scope
var _hsb_afp = &default_alloc;  # the Allocator getter: default_alloc, or _hsb_scope_vt under a scope

# The scope's vtable for the stdlib's allocator-taking calls (vec_new_a, vec_push_a,
# str_from_a, str_builder_*_a): the caller's allocator, its allocations zeroed. Static
# storage, never the heap: alloc_reset() would scrub a vtable allocated there (the stdlib
# keeps default_alloc()'s in static storage for the same reason). Slot 4, the state, is the
# caller's allocator; the four functions read it from their state argument.
var _hsb_vt: i64[5];

# Zero n bytes at p: whole words, then the bytes left.
fn _hsb_zero(p, n) {
    var w = n / 8;
    for (var i = 0; i < w; i = i + 1) { store64(p + i * 8, 0); }
    for (var j = w * 8; j < n; j = j + 1) { store8(p + j, 0); }
    return 0;
}

# The scope's alloc: n bytes of the caller's allocator al, zeroed; 0 as alloc gives it.
fn _hsb_zalloc(al, n) {
    if (n <= 0 || n > ALLOC_MAX) { return 0; }
    var p = alloc_via(al, n);
    if (p != 0) { _hsb_zero(p, n); }
    return p;
}

# The scope's realloc: the caller's, the grown tail zeroed.
fn _hsb_zrealloc(al, old_ptr, old_size, new_size) {
    if (new_size <= 0 || new_size > ALLOC_MAX) { return 0; }
    var p = realloc_via(al, old_ptr, old_size, new_size);
    if (p != 0 && new_size > old_size) { _hsb_zero(p + old_size, new_size - old_size); }
    return p;
}

# The scope's free and reset: the caller's own.
fn _hsb_free(al, ptr) { return free_via(al, ptr); }
fn _hsb_reset(al) { return reset_via(al); }

# hisab_alloc's target under a scope, and hisab_allocator's.
fn _hsb_scoped(n) { return _hsb_zalloc(_hsb_al, n); }
fn _hsb_scope_vt() { return &_hsb_vt; }

# Route every allocation hisab makes from now on to the Allocator al (lib/alloc.cyr's
# vtable), or, for al = 0, to the global bump heap, as through 3.4.1. Returns the scope it
# replaces (an Allocator, or 0), so a caller restores it: `var prev = hisab_alloc_scope(al);
# ...; hisab_alloc_scope(prev);`. See the block above for what the scope covers and what a
# reset of al invalidates. al is not called here, only stored; it must stay valid while it
# is the scope.
public fn hisab_alloc_scope(al) {
    var prev = _hsb_al;
    if (al != 0) {
        var vt = &_hsb_vt;
        store64(vt, &_hsb_zalloc);
        store64(vt + 8, &_hsb_zrealloc);
        store64(vt + 16, &_hsb_free);
        store64(vt + 24, &_hsb_reset);
        store64(vt + 32, al);
        _hsb_fp = &_hsb_scoped;
        _hsb_afp = &_hsb_scope_vt;
    } else {
        _hsb_fp = &alloc;
        _hsb_afp = &default_alloc;
    }
    _hsb_al = al;
    return prev;
}

# The Allocator in scope, or 0 when hisab allocates from the global bump heap.
public fn hisab_alloc_scope_current() { return _hsb_al; }

# n bytes for hisab: `alloc(n)` with no scope; with one, n bytes of the scope's allocator,
# zeroed, as alloc's are. 0 for n <= 0, n > ALLOC_MAX and a refusal. Every allocation in
# hisab's source goes through this.
#inline
public fn hisab_alloc(n) { return fncall1(_hsb_fp, n); }

# The Allocator hisab hands the stdlib's allocator-taking calls: default_alloc(), the global
# bump, with no scope; with one, the scope's (its allocations zeroed).
#inline
public fn hisab_allocator() { return fncall0(_hsb_afp); }

# A new empty vec (vec_new's), its header and buffer from hisab_allocator(); 0 when refused.
#inline
public fn hisab_vec_new() { return vec_new_a(fncall0(_hsb_afp)); }
